hostname.description:
A hostname pattern used to match incoming requests.

This is the *user-facing* representation that gets stored in configuration
files and CLI arguments.  Use [`HostName::to_host_pattern`] to materialise
it into a [`HostPattern`] suitable for the proxy routing layer.

# Syntax examples

- `test`  → turns into `test.<assigned-fqdn>`
- `@`     → the bare assigned FQDN (default route)
- `*`     → accepts **any** hostname (not recommended)
- `*.your-domain.com` → any subdomain of `your-domain.com`
- `*.sub` → any subdomain of `sub.<assigned-fqdn>`

# Constructing

```rust
use cruma_tunnels_lib::hostname::HostName;

// From a literal:
let a = HostName::new("myapp");

// From any Into<String>:
let b: HostName = "myapp".into();

// Wrapping a String directly (the inner field is public):
let c = HostName(String::from("myapp"));

assert_eq!(a, b);
assert_eq!(b, c);
```

port.description:
Local listener port to probe. A real loopback TCP connection is made to `127.0.0.1:<port>`,
so this must be a bound, HTTP-terminating listener (plaintext HTTP or TLS-terminating HTTPS;
TLS-passthrough and raw-TCP listeners cannot answer the probe).
